wer-9642056c-e195-5a5d-999d-cbb62ad9ecd5" "answer-3cfeaa67-8697-54cc-9fc1-de86d0fed88b" "answer-343556ba-5530-5b15-a965-c8c027ad0298" "answer-ffa60599-8ada-5f0a-8923-8955ff0349e7" "answer-fd23fd1d-9f28-5c10-aa6e-819c6f2197f3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9642056c-e195-5a5d-999d-cbb62ad9ecd5" :answer/id #uuid "9642056c-e195-5a5d-999d-cbb62ad9ecd5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(R∧Q)∧¬P" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3cfeaa67-8697-54cc-9fc1-de86d0fed88b" :answer/id #uuid "3cfeaa67-8697-54cc-9fc1-de86d0fed88b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(R∧Q)∨¬P" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-343556ba-5530-5b15-a965-c8c027ad0298" :answer/id #uuid "343556ba-5530-5b15-a965-c8c027ad0298" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "P∨(R∧Q)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ffa60599-8ada-5f0a-8923-8955ff0349e7" :answer/id #uuid "ffa60599-8ada-5f0a-8923-8955ff0349e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "P∨(¬R∧Q)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fd23fd1d-9f28-5c10-aa6e-819c6f2197f3" :answer/id #uuid "fd23fd1d-9f28-5c10-aa6e-819c6f2197f3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "¬P∨(R∨Q)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179693 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ba2fee1b-3766-585f-9ea4-855819a270ac"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-ba2fee1b-3766-585f-9ea4-855819a270ac" :question/id #uuid "ba2fee1b-3766-585f-9ea4-855819a270ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-163546" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the positive $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ the magnitude of the acceleration is $\\frac{k}{x+1}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $k$ is a positive constant, and the acceleration is directed toward $O.$ If $P$ has velocity $v=4\\,m/s$ at the origin and the particle is at rest when its displacement is $({e}^{4}-1)\\,m,$ find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/e9/e938c4ba0e20c0714d1cd0ef62fb6596b37341b06d29de4e675981007610e12a.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $\\frac{k}{x+1}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-\\frac{k}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (-\\frac{k}{x+1})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=-k\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=4\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =-k\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & =-k\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0+1)+C \\\\ C & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=8-k\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1).\n$$\n\nFinally, since $v=0\\,m/s$ when $x=({e}^{4}-1)\\,m,$ we can find the value of the positive constant $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =8-k\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2} & =8-k\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({e}^{4}-1)+1) \\\\ k\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{4}) & =8 \\\\ 4k & =8 \\\\ k & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-07559b22-9501-5406-b1d4-af6af28cc58e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-07559b22-9501-5406-b1d4-af6af28cc58e" :answer-field/id #uuid "07559b22-9501-5406-b1d4-af6af28cc58e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7fa15e1a-d450-5bdf-ab2e-7db7463df9ae" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7fa15e1a-d450-5bdf-ab2e-7db7463df9ae" "answer-b0866692-8ed1-5f90-8e94